        "column_1_align_desktop": "center",
        "column_1_html": "<h2>I got Oxmo when I was 18</h2><p>It was January 2017. I was in the middle of transferring from college in Arizona back to Los Angeles when I drove up to the Bay Area to bring him home. He was eight weeks old. Just this tiny golden retriever puppy who looked like a little perfect potato.</p><p>For just under six years, he became the best part of almost everything I did.</p><p>Oxmo was beautiful, built like an Olympian, and somehow carried himself with this very serious, presidential energy. My dad said, \"This dog has class.\" I called him President.</p><p>He was incredibly smart. If he wanted a treat, he'd point his nose toward it. If he wanted to play, he brought me his ball. If he wanted to go somewhere, he showed me his leash and pawed at the front door.</p><p>And every time I came home, he greeted me like I'd been gone for ten years.</p><p>He slept beside me every night. Whenever anyone in the house was sick or hurting, he glued himself to their side. If I was sad, he felt it too. He was my shadow, my best friend, and the first dog I ever went out into the world and chose for myself.</p>\n<p class=\"emphasized-text\">He was exactly the dog I'd always dreamed of.</p>",
        "column_1_image_first": "image_first",
