        "column_2_html": "<div class=\"why-this-matters-container\" style=\"opacity: 1; transform: none\">\n  <h2>September 2022</h2>\n  <div class=\"space-y-4 font-sans text-[#636E72] leading-relaxed\">\n    <p>\n      Then, in September of 2022, I lost him suddenly in a tragic accident. There was no warning.\n    </p>\n    <p>\n      Nothing prepared me for what came after. The guilt. Waking up and realizing that the routines we'd repeated thousands of times were gone. That he was gone. The world seemed to expect me to return to normal, but for a long time, I couldn't. I lost family.\n    </p>\n    <p>\n      For seven nights after he died, friends and family came over and just sat with me. That became my way of mourning him, and later a chapter in the guide.\n    </p>\n    <p>\n      I volunteered hundreds of hours at the West Los Angeles Animal Shelter. It healed me in ways I didn't expect.\n    </p>\n    <p>\n      What helped most was writing. Journaling, and working through grief prompts out loud. I realized that if I could turn even a small part of that pain into something that helped another person feel less alone, then something good could keep coming from Oxmo's life. That practice became The Dog Grief Guide.\n    </p>\n    <p>\n      About a year later, I brought Ness home. I looked for a puppy from Oxmo's family on purpose, because that connection meant a lot to me. He didn't replace him. He just opened my heart back up and gave me somewhere to put all my love.\n    </p>\n    <p>\n      I still think about Oxmo every day. I still cry, but I smile more now.\n    </p>\n    <p>\n      I want Oxmo to be remembered not for the way he died, but for the way he lived. And I want that for your dog too.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n  <div>\n    <a class=\"explore-the-guide-link\" href=\"/products/the-dog-grief-guide-1\"><span>Get the Guide</span><span class=\"explore-the-guide-arrow\">→</span></a>\n  </div>\n</div>",
